A||||5||wreakhavoc||Removing front brake rotor||||1||
Z||000000||wreakhavoc||07-10-2001||04:40 PM||||I hate metric nuts and bolts! What is the socket size needed to remove the big nut on the spindle that holds on the rotor and wheel bearings?<BR>'86 SE V-6/4-speed ||||reg||1
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||07-10-2001||05:06 PM||||not really sure what size that bolt is .But after you pull the cotter pin out of the hole in the spindle,you should be able to use a pair of channel lock pliers to unloosen that nut I've never used a wrench or socket of any kind to remove that nut .on any car<p>[This message has been edited by starfighter007@msn.com (edited 07-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Leadfoot||07-10-2001||05:23 PM||||O! I new this one.  I think it was something like a 32mm.  I have the socket at home.  Anyway, call-up Napa or Pep boys, they should be able to tell you the right size.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DecadenceR||07-10-2001||05:54 PM||||If it's tight enough that you need a socket, you could have problems....<BR>Any one I've ever seen is just taken off with channel locks, usually it's just a little more than hand tight.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||07-10-2001||07:38 PM||||It shouldn't be very tight at all, but I find a socket is still the easiest way to remove it. Its a 24mm nut.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||07-11-2001||12:41 PM||||You might as well learn to like metric nuts and bolts becaus everything except a couple of bolts on a Fiero is metric.<P>AutoZone (and probably others) has a tool loaner program.  You pay a deposit when you take the tool.  You get it back when you return it.  They have all kinds of specialty tools as well as common ones.<p>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 07-11-2001).]||||reg||1
